    "body": "Çöpler sulphide project. ( Image courtesy of Anagold Madencilik. )\nBy: Staff Writer November 27, 2026\nThe Çöpler gold mine in eastern Turkey has resumed processing stockpiled ore more than two years after a heap leach failure killed nine workers, Bloomberg reported Wednesday.\nOperator Anagold Madencilik began treating the ore at its pressure oxidation plant in the third quarter under a temporary operating licence, a company spokesperson told the news agency.\nAnagold aims to produce about 1.3 tonnes of gold by year-end, equivalent to roughly 41,800 ounces. The mine produced 6.9 tonnes in 2023, or about a fifth of Turkey’s total output that year.\nThe restart comes as Turkey seeks to expand domestic gold production and reduce its dependence on imports. Energy and Natural Resources Minister Alparslan Bayraktar told a parliamentary committee Nov. 23 that the government’s goal is annual production of 100 tonnes. He gave no deadline for the target.\nThe resumption follows the exit of SSR Mining (TSX, Nasdaq: SSRM), which is based in the United States. The company completed the sale of its 80% stake in Çöpler and related properties to Turkish conglomerate Cengiz Holding and affiliates on June 24 for about $1.5 billion (C$2.1 billion) in cash after adjustments. Turkey’s Lidya Madencilik holds the remaining 20%.\nEnvironmental fallout\nThe February 2024 heap leach failure sent material into the nearby Sabırlı Valley and Manganese Pit, burying nine workers. All nine bodies had been recovered by June that year, according to SSR.\nTurkish authorities revoked the mine’s environmental permit after the disaster. SSR spent $149.3 million on reclamation and remediation at the site through the end of 2025.\nThe landslide also raised concerns about potential cyanide contamination of the Euphrates River. Turkey’s deputy environment minister told parliament in May 2024 that surface and groundwater analyses had found no adverse effects from cyanide.\nThe disaster marked the second major operational disruption at Çöpler in less than two years. In June 2022, Turkey’s environment ministry ordered a production halt after a pipeline leaked diluted cyanide solution.\nLegal proceedings over the fatal landslide continue. A trial involving 43 defendants accused of causing deaths and injuries through negligence last convened Oct. 20. The court scheduled the next hearing for Jan. 26, 2027, while awaiting a new expert report, Turkish news outlet Bianet reported. Print\nTAG: gold mines gold mining gold production\nCOMMODITY: Gold\nREGION: Europe\nCOMPANY: Cengiz Holding\nRepublish this article",
